WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.040
Steve Kaplan: After the Normal World you have the What the F*ck moment.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.540
The WTF Moment.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.160
And that is the essence of the comic premise.

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:17.890
The comic premise is the idea that that something impossible or improbable happens, impossible

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:22.970
in the sense of a man living the same day
over and over again that’s impossible.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:32.500
Improbable that a somewhat out of shape, not too physical analyst could be put out to the

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:38.790
field and do the derring-do that James Bond
would that is not impossible but isn’t improbable

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:39.790
[referring to SPY Movie].

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:47.300
So all comic premises revolve around the thought
that this impossible or improbable moment

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:54.710
happens and it asks the question what would
happen then and the best comedies then develop

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:58.750
that premise through character.

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:05.279
Paul Feig talks about how he wrote the script
for SPY focusing not on what funny things

00:01:05.279 --> 00:01:10.770
he could do but what comic moments would come
out of character of simply putting Melissa

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:17.789
McCarthy’s character in these perilous situations
and then seeing how she would cope, how she

00:01:17.789 --> 00:01:25.999
would grow, how she would become not only
a better person but a more fully realized

00:01:25.999 --> 00:01:27.630
person of who she is.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:37.179
So after you have the WTF moment, you have
what we call the Reaction.

00:01:37.179 --> 00:01:46.200
Now in most comedies when the character is
faced with a life-changing, life-altering

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.789
impossible situation, most characters desperately
try to claw back to the normal world.

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:59.139
Now remember, in the Normal World your characters
are misshapen.

00:01:59.139 --> 00:02:05.679
Your protagonists are not who they want to
be or need to be but they don’t know it.

00:02:05.679 --> 00:02:09.739
They are unaware of it.

00:02:09.739 --> 00:02:15.680
They turn themselves into a pretzel to try
to convince themselves that they’re having

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:16.680
a happy life.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:23.459
That their life is O.K. and when the comic
premise occurs, that is all taken away from

00:02:23.459 --> 00:02:24.459
them.

00:02:24.459 --> 00:02:26.709
The rug is literally pulled out from under
their feet.

00:02:26.709 --> 00:02:35.900
So in most Reaction sections there is a large
part of action that is denial.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:44.909
The character is desperately trying to go
back to the way they were before this occurred.

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:53.640
In TROPIC THUNDER for instance the WTF moment
is Steve Coogan (the director) has taken Robert

00:02:53.640 --> 00:03:01.719
Downey, Jr. and Ben Stiller and all these
other actors out into the country and the

00:03:01.719 --> 00:03:07.379
jungle because they’re making a Vietnam-era
movie and he wants them to experience the

00:03:07.379 --> 00:03:17.660
country (Nam) and he accidentally steps on
a landmine and blows himself up.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:24.030
And while some of the actors (especially Jay
Baruchel) think “This isn’t part of the

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:25.030
movie.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:26.030
This is not right.”

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:32.159
Ben Stiller, the protagonist of the movie,
is convinced that it is simply part of the

00:03:32.159 --> 00:03:39.819
director plan and he keeps on trying to go
along and keep filming this Vietnam era movie.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:44.499
So he’s in denial that this has happened.

00:03:44.499 --> 00:03:50.769
In GROUNDHOG DAY, Bill Murray spends the first
day that he has relived in denial.

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:53.989
He says “Is this still Groundhog’s day?”

00:03:53.989 --> 00:04:02.660
It’s only until the third day when he finally
starts to then figure out what’s happening

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:04.280
and how he can deal with it.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:10.790
So the first part of reaction is usually denial
but the second part of reaction is starting

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:18.389
to cope with the new situation but without
any character development or personality development

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:21.789
or personality change.

00:04:21.789 --> 00:04:28.880
For instance, Bill Murray in Groundhog Day
is kind of a jerk, so when he realizes that

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.139
whatever he does will have no consequences
for him he still is a jerk.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:39.030
He figures out that he can go talk to a girl
and get some information from her.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:45.170
He can come back the next day, he’ll remember
all the conversation but she won’t and he

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:46.960
can pick her up and have sex with her.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:52.260
And thereby he has sex with all the women
in Punxsutawney after a time.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:56.960
So you can see that he is dealing with the
situation but really without any character

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.590
growth.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:09.720
For Melissa McCarthy, her reaction is both
pain and sorrow because she loved Jude Law

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:16.790
but it’s also anger and the sense that she
has to avenge his death.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:24.870
Jude Law gave her this terrible piece of jewelry
that he put on a lanyard, a crazy cupcake

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:31.370
that he gave to Melissa McCarthy and of course
it’s a terrible idea to give this to a girl

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:34.300
that is in love with you.

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:45.569
But it becomes very important, symbolic touchstones,
tangible touchstones are very important because

00:05:45.569 --> 00:05:48.080
you can say a lot with a little.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:56.919
So at his funeral or the memorial service
for him at the CIA you can see Melissa McCarthy

00:05:56.919 --> 00:06:04.139
taking out crazy cupcake ceramic kind of touching
it and you could tell that she’s trying

00:06:04.139 --> 00:06:09.640
to hold onto Bradley Fine played by Jude Law.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:16.010
In another scene at the CIA when they realize
that everybody’s cover has been blown and

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:23.270
there is this nuclear bomb on the loose but
none of her regular spies like Jason Statham’s

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:28.039
Rick Ford can go after it.

00:06:28.039 --> 00:06:34.700
Melissa McCarthy takes out the crazy cupcake,
holds onto it and quite out of character jumps

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.680
out and says “I can go!

00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:39.250
They don’t know me.”

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:46.180
Her reaction is that, even though she’s
scared and even though she really doesn’t

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:49.340
have any faith in herself.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:55.440
Because of her great love for Bradley Fine/Jude
Law, she’s still going to do it.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:02.889
So reaction is the moment when characters
are still trying to deal with this impossible

00:07:02.889 --> 00:07:09.430
or improbable set of circumstances that they’re
now trying to deal with and what happens in

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:18.289
this sequence is your characters are given
simple tasks and missions to try to get where

00:07:18.289 --> 00:07:21.710
they’re going to go or do what they’re
going to do.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:28.810
For Melissa McCarthy in SPY, first she is
sent to Paris and all she has to do is go

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:37.139
into the office and spy on this bad guy who’s
going to lead her to other bad guys who are

00:07:37.139 --> 00:07:39.240
going to lead her to the nuclear bomb.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.920
It’s that simple.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.460
She doesn’t have to become a super spy to
do that.

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:49.650
But because they’re on to her and because
they have blown up the office building where

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:56.860
she was supposed to be, one thing leads to
another and there’s a series of discrete

00:07:56.860 --> 00:08:04.969
missions or tasks that she felt that she follows
one after the other that lead her on the path

00:08:04.969 --> 00:08:14.849
to trying to recover the bomb, get the bad
guys and then avenge her love, Jude Law and

00:08:14.849 --> 00:08:22.860
all the while she is doing this without her
realizing it, she’s changing because what

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:31.789
happens in a comedy (and you can argue almost
any movie, but especially in comedies) because

00:08:31.789 --> 00:08:37.361
you’re starting with characters who are
more than flawed, they’re broken, there

00:08:37.361 --> 00:08:39.290
is something wrong with them.

00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:43.580
You can absolutely see there’s something
wrong Ben Stiller in Tropic Thunder because

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:44.870
he’s an idiot.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:49.370
There is something wrong with Bill Murray
in Groundhog Day, he’s a jerk!

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:54.980
There is something wrong with Susan Cooper,
Melissa McCarthy in SPY because she’s got

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:56.610
no confidence.

00:08:56.610 --> 00:08:59.380
She has no sense of who she is.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:09.180
And because of this impossible situation that
they’re now going to have to react to our

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:11.460
characters that are transformed.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:16.580
In a romantic comedy, it’s love that transforms
a character.

00:09:16.580 --> 00:09:23.080
You know a psychiatrist will tell you that
if you marry a jerk, 30 years later you have

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.590
an older, fatter, balder jerk.

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:33.690
But in romantic comedies love is a magical
power that is transformative.

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:39.160
In Groundhog Day, it transforms Bill Murray
from a jerk into a really good guy.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:45.160
And yes, he has to live through several lifetimes
to get to be able to do that but still…For

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:52.260
Melissa McCarthy in SPY what’s transformative
is even though she’s not aware of it, she’s

00:09:52.260 --> 00:10:01.200
been able to come up with new powers, new
skills, and in reaction, is part of the movie

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:07.600
in which new skills that have been hinted
at in the normal world, new skills start to

00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:08.600
appear.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:09.600
QUESTION: What is the “all is lost: moment in Groundhog Day and at what point in the

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:10.100
movie does it occur?

